WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.122
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.122 --> 00:00:11.580
In this short
section, you're going

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.890
to look at the weak
interaction a little bit more,

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.470
and specifically discuss
neutral currents.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.820
We looked in some detail
at charged currents--

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.820
specifically, the
interaction with quarks.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.670
So here, I'm going to look
at the Z boson specifically,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:31.950
and the weak interaction
via the neutral current.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.350
So studying those
two processes here,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.580
where there is an
electron and a positron

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.380
through some process including
a Z boson and a photon

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.050
and resulting in a
muon and an anti-muon--

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.480
those processes have been
studied in great detail

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.950
at SLAC and at CERN, at the SLC,
and the Large Electron-Positron

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.300
Collider.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.620
So if we want to calculate
the cross-section

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.220
and study the cross-section
of the center of mass energy,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.610
we see a number of
interesting effects.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.730
At low energies, and
at very large energies,

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:10.170
the cross-section runs with
1 over the energy squared.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.340
But at the mass of the Z boson,
we see this enormous resonance

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.500
here.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.890
The cross-section at the
resonance from the Z boson

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.600
is about 200 times that
of just a photon exchange.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.490
So this allows you to study the
Z boson with great precision

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.510
at those colliders.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.610
You have sizable
cross-section when you are

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.530
in electron-positron colliders.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.120
And then you can,
with precision,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.732
look at, what is the rate
into a muon/anti-muon?

00:01:38.732 --> 00:01:40.440
What is the rate into
a quark/anti-quark?

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.110
And so on.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:45.390
And you can study the mass,
the width of the Z boson

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.930
with an enormous
level of precision.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.450
Again, so I will not go
into too much detail here.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.710
And please have a look at
chapter 9.6 in Griffiths,

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:56.730
for example.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.520
But there's many other resources
where you can learn more

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.040
about [? neutral ?] currents.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.800
Neutral currents,
electroweak neutral currents

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.190
are specifically important
in the study of neutrinos,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:13.760
as we will discuss more
in the lectures as well.

